 ## Overview
-Tetrahedral Volume Rendering (TetVR) is a cell-projection method that renders unstructured tetrahedral meshes by projecting individual tetrahedra onto the screen and compositing them in depth order [S3]. It solves the problem of accurately visualizing complex, non-uniform volumetric data (such as finite element analysis results or dark matter simulations) where regular grid-based ray marching fails or introduces sampling artifacts [S5, unofficial]. Unlike [Projected Tetrahedra](877-projected-tetrahedra.md) which often relies on explicit isosurface extraction or simpler blending, TetVR supports arbitrary isosurface values and partial pre-integration directly within the projection pipeline, enabling hybrid volume/isosurface visualization [S2]. It differs from [Exact Visibility Ordering for Cell Projection Volume Rendering (SXMPVO)](944-exact-visibility-ordering-for-cell-projection-volume-renderi.md) by using hardware rasterization and screen-space classification to determine visibility order, trading exact topological correctness for GPU acceleration [S3].
+Tetrahedral Volume Rendering (TetVR) is a cell-projection method that renders unstructured tetrahedral meshes by projecting individual tetrahedra onto the screen and compositing them in depth order [S3]. It solves the problem of accurately visualizing complex, non-uniform volumetric data (such as finite element analysis results or dark matter simulations) where regular grid-based ray marching fails or introduces sampling artifacts [S5, unofficial]. Unlike [Projected Tetrahedra](877-projected-tetrahedra.md) which often relies on explicit isosurface extraction or simpler blending, this method supports arbitrary isosurface values via density addition [S2] and partial pre-integration for color computation [S3], enabling hybrid volume/isosurface visualization. It differs from [Exact Visibility Ordering for Cell Projection Volume Rendering (SXMPVO)](944-exact-visibility-ordering-for-cell-projection-volume-renderi.md) by using hardware rasterization and screen-space classification to determine visibility order, trading exact topological correctness for GPU acceleration [S3].
 
